const blogContent = {
  "maasai-culture-safari-east-africa": [
    { type: "text", content: "A Maasai warrior in a red shuka, standing against the evening light, is one of East Africa's most recognizable safari images. It is also only the beginning of the story. The Maasai are not a backdrop to wildlife travel. They are a living, resilient people whose history, land knowledge, beadwork, songs, cattle traditions and community values continue to shape Kenya and Tanzania today." },
    { type: "text", content: "For culture-focused travelers, a thoughtful Maasai encounter can become one of the most memorable parts of a safari. It asks you to slow down, listen carefully, and meet people as hosts, guides, artists, conservation partners and neighbors to the wildlife landscapes you have come to see." },
    { type: "quote", content: "A respectful cultural safari is not about collecting photographs. It is about entering a conversation with humility, curiosity and time." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Who Are the Maasai?" },
    { type: "text", content: "The Maasai are a Nilotic people whose ancestral movement followed the Nile Valley into what is now southern Kenya and northern Tanzania. Today, Maasai communities live across the Serengeti-Mara ecosystem, Amboseli, the Rift Valley, Ngorongoro, Loliondo, Laikipia and many other landscapes that are central to East African safari travel." },
    { type: "text", content: "Their name is often understood through the Maa language idea of <em>Il Maasai</em>, or my people. Within that shared identity are many clans, local histories and ways of adapting to modern life. Some Maasai live in traditional homesteads; others are teachers, entrepreneurs, politicians, conservationists, lodge managers, university students and professional guides. The culture is not frozen in time. It is alive, adapting and proudly carried forward." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/maasai-cultural-experience-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Maasai cultural encounter during an East African safari", caption: "Maasai cultural encounters are most meaningful when they are guided by respect, context and genuine exchange." },
    { type: "h2", content: "The Manyatta: What a Village Visit Can Include" },
    { type: "text", content: "A village visit, often to a manyatta or enkang, usually begins with a community host who explains the layout of the homestead. Traditional houses are built by women using sticks, mud, grass and cow dung, arranged around a central cattle enclosure. That cattle enclosure is not decorative. Cattle remain deeply tied to wealth, nourishment, identity and spiritual life." },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Welcome songs:</strong> a communal introduction that sets the tone for the visit.",
      "<strong>Home tour:</strong> a chance to understand architecture, family life and daily rhythms.",
      "<strong>Fire-making demonstration:</strong> a practical skill that connects guests with bush knowledge.",
      "<strong>Adumu jumping dance:</strong> an age-set performance by young warriors, often with visitors invited to try.",
      "<strong>Beadwork display:</strong> jewelry made by Maasai women, with color, pattern and status carrying meaning.",
    ]},
    { type: "text", content: "Some villages charge a small entrance fee, and many sell beadwork or crafts. That is not a flaw in the experience. When handled transparently, the income supports households, women's economic independence and local reasons to preserve cultural knowledge. What matters is scale and dignity: smaller community-run visits with patient guides usually feel more personal than large bus-stop performances." },
    { type: "h2", content: "The Adumu Jumping Dance" },
    { type: "text", content: "The <strong>adumu</strong>, often called the Maasai jumping dance, is performed by young Maasai warriors, or <em>moran</em>. Men form a circle, chant in rhythmic unison, and take turns leaping straight up from a standing position. The height of the jump brings admiration, but the dance is also about age-set unity, strength, discipline and ceremony." },
    { type: "text", content: "If you are invited to join, accept with good humor. It is much harder than it looks. More importantly, take a moment to appreciate the athleticism and cultural meaning instead of treating the dance as a quick photo opportunity." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Maasai Beadwork: A Language of Color" },
    { type: "text", content: "Beadwork is one of the richest visual languages in Maasai culture. Before glass beads arrived through trade, ornaments used natural materials such as seeds, shells, bone, clay and copper. Today, glass beads create bold geometric necklaces, bracelets, belts, earrings and ceremonial pieces. Women are the primary artists, and jewelry can signal age, marital status, family position, ceremony and personal beauty." },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Red:</strong> bravery, strength and unity, associated with blood, warriors and resilience.",
      "<strong>Blue:</strong> the sky, rain, energy and Enkai, the divine provider.",
      "<strong>Green:</strong> land, health, pasture and prosperity.",
      "<strong>White:</strong> milk, peace, purity and nourishment.",
      "<strong>Black:</strong> the people, unity through hardship and the realities of life.",
      "<strong>Orange:</strong> hospitality, friendship and generosity toward guests.",
      "<strong>Yellow:</strong> fertility, the sun and abundance.",
      "<strong>Striped patterns:</strong> the earth, territory and the layered landscapes of home.",
    ]},
    { type: "quote", content: "Every bead color has a voice: the sky, the land, the cattle, the sun, the people and the welcome offered to a guest." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Greetings That Open Doors" },
    { type: "text", content: "Learning even a few Maa words changes the tone of a visit. Try <strong>Supa</strong> for hello, <strong>Erokamena</strong> for thank you, <strong>Iyakoko</strong> when asking how a group is, <strong>Keyiolong</strong> for good morning, and <strong>Olee sere</strong> for goodbye to one person. Many Maasai hosts in tourism areas also speak Swahili and English, but the effort to greet in Maa is usually received with warmth." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Maasai Communities and Wildlife Conservation" },
    { type: "text", content: "The Maasai relationship with wildlife is often misunderstood. Traditionally, Maasai pastoralists did not hunt wildlife for food, and the land was used communally for grazing. Historic lion killing existed as a regulated warrior rite, but modern conservation programs have helped shift that story toward wildlife guardianship, compensation schemes, ranger work and community conservancies." },
    { type: "text", content: "This matters because many of East Africa's most important wildlife corridors cross Maasai-owned or Maasai-used land. Amboseli, the Maasai Mara and the Serengeti do not function as isolated islands. Elephants, lions, wildebeest and zebra all depend on dispersal areas beyond park boundaries. When Maasai communities benefit fairly from conservation and tourism, wildlife has more space to survive." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Where to Experience Maasai Culture" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Maasai Mara, Kenya:</strong> the most accessible option, especially near Talek, Sekenani and Aitong. Choose smaller visits with guides who have real community relationships.",
      "<strong>Amboseli, Kenya:</strong> Maasai communities live around the park boundary, with Kilimanjaro and elephant country as a powerful setting.",
      "<strong>Laikipia and Loita Hills, Kenya:</strong> excellent for lower-volume cultural encounters connected to conservancy life and pastoral landscapes.",
      "<strong>Ngorongoro, Tanzania:</strong> Maasai communities live within the wider conservation area, where cattle, culture and wildlife share a complex landscape.",
      "<strong>Loliondo, Monduli and Longido, Tanzania:</strong> rewarding choices for travelers who want less commercialized cultural tourism near the northern circuit.",
    ]},
    { type: "text", content: "Our <a href=\"/itineraries#kenya-family-8\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">8-Day Kenya Family Safari</a> can include Maasai village time in the Mara, while the <a href=\"/itineraries#kenya-tanzania-family-11\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">11-Day Kenya-Tanzania Family Safari</a> carries families through Maasai lands on both sides of the border. Tanzania-focused guests can pair Ngorongoro and Serengeti wildlife with cultural context on our <a href=\"/itineraries#tanzania-family-8\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">8-Day Tanzania Family Safari</a> or add a romantic cultural evening to the <a href=\"/itineraries#tanzania-honeymoon-8\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">8-Day Tanzania Honeymoon Safari</a>." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Cultural Etiquette for a Respectful Visit" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Dress modestly, especially when entering homes or community spaces.",
      "Ask before photographing people, homes, ceremonies or children.",
      "Listen more than you speak, and let elders or hosts set the pace.",
      "Buy beadwork directly from women when possible, and avoid aggressive haggling.",
      "Do not hand out sweets to children; ask your guide what support is appropriate.",
      "Participate when invited, but never pressure anyone for a performance or pose.",
      "Avoid treating village life as poverty tourism. Meet people with dignity and curiosity.",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "The Maasai Today" },
    { type: "text", content: "Modern Maasai life holds tradition and change together. Education, land subdivision, climate pressure, tourism income, conservation work and business opportunities all shape daily decisions. Some families maintain cattle-centered pastoral life. Others balance herding with school calendars, lodge jobs, market trade or professional careers." },
    { type: "text", content: "That complexity is exactly why respectful cultural tourism matters. A good visit does not ask the Maasai to perform an old version of themselves for travelers. It creates space for hosts to explain who they are now, what they value, what is changing, and what they want guests to understand." },
    { type: "h2", content: "A Gentle Invitation" },
    { type: "text", content: "A safari is more than wildlife. It is also the people who know the land by seasons, songs, cattle paths and family stories. Done well, a Maasai cultural encounter adds depth to game drives, gives children a human connection to East Africa, and helps honeymooners or returning safari guests experience the region with richer context." },
    { type: "text", content: "Ready to go beyond the Big Five and connect with the people who have called these landscapes home for centuries? Contact Sheila and Violet at <strong>Simbali Wild Safaris</strong>. We will craft an itinerary that pairs extraordinary wildlife with Maasai cultural encounters that are respectful, enriching and unforgettable. <a href=\"/contact\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">Start planning your cultural safari</a>." },
  ],
  "lake-naivasha-kenya-boat-safari-birdwatching": [
    { type: "text", content: "At dawn on <strong>Lake Naivasha</strong>, the safari begins softly. Mist lifts from the freshwater. Papyrus edges glow silver-green. Somewhere across the lake, an African fish eagle calls, and a pod of hippos breaks the surface with low, contented snorts." },
    { type: "text", content: "This is a different rhythm from the open plains. On Naivasha, water replaces dust, reflections replace horizons, and the birds often steal the show from the big cats. It is one of Kenya's most restorative safari pauses: calm enough for families and honeymooners, rich enough for serious birdwatchers, and perfectly placed between the drama of Samburu, Ol Pejeta, Amboseli, Lake Nakuru and the Maasai Mara." },
    { type: "quote", content: "Lake Naivasha is where a Kenyan safari exhales: boat safaris at sunrise, fish eagles overhead, and hippos resting in the shallows." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Why Lake Naivasha Feels So Different" },
    { type: "text", content: "Lake Naivasha sits at about <strong>1,884 meters above sea level</strong>, making it Kenya's highest major Rift Valley lake. Unlike many of the alkaline lakes nearby, Naivasha is freshwater, fed by underground springs from the Aberdare Mountains. That freshwater character supports a rich mosaic of papyrus swamp, acacia woodland, submerged vegetation, grazing lawns and open water." },
    { type: "text", content: "The lake has no obvious surface outlet. Water leaves through evaporation and underground seepage, and its levels have risen and fallen over the years, reshaping shorelines and wildlife patterns. Around it, you will find Mount Longonot, Eburru Forest, Hell's Gate National Park and Kenya's flower-growing heartland, where rose farms add a complex conservation and livelihood story to the landscape." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/serene-lakeside-mountain-landscape_blog.jpg", alt: "Peaceful lakeside landscape with mountains and still water", caption: "Lake Naivasha offers a slower, water-led safari rhythm within Kenya's Rift Valley." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Birdwatching: 400+ Species on the Water's Edge" },
    { type: "text", content: "For bird lovers, Naivasha is a destination in its own right. More than <strong>400 bird species</strong> have been recorded around the lake, from conspicuous waterbirds to papyrus specialists that reward patient eyes and good binoculars." },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>African fish eagle:</strong> the signature sound of Naivasha, often seen perched in lakeside trees or diving toward the water.",
      "<strong>Pelicans and herons:</strong> great white pelicans, pink-backed pelicans, goliath herons, purple herons and black herons are frequent highlights.",
      "<strong>Kingfishers:</strong> pied, malachite, giant and woodland kingfishers bring flashes of blue and copper along the shore.",
      "<strong>Waders and migrants:</strong> stilts, avocets, sandpipers, plovers and Palearctic migrants add variety between October and April.",
      "<strong>Papyrus specialists:</strong> papyrus gonolek, white-winged warbler and Carruthers's cisticola draw dedicated birders into the reedbeds.",
    ]},
    { type: "image", src: "/images/raptor-bird-flying-clear-sky_blog.jpg", alt: "African raptor soaring in a clear sky", caption: "The African fish eagle's call is one of Naivasha's defining safari sounds." },
    { type: "h2", content: "The Boat Safari: Naivasha's Essential Experience" },
    { type: "text", content: "A guided <strong>Lake Naivasha boat safari</strong> is the experience we would build the day around. Most trips last one to two hours, with sunrise and late afternoon offering the loveliest light. Local guides know the hippo territories, eagle perches, pelican roosts and quiet corners where waterbuck graze close to the shore." },
    { type: "text", content: "Hippos are viewed from a respectful distance. They may look serene, but they are powerful, territorial animals, and good guiding matters. From the boat, guests can photograph them safely at water level, watch cormorants drying their wings on submerged branches, and see fish eagles patrol the lake edge." },
    { type: "text", content: "For photographers, the boat is a gift: low angles, mirrored water, moving subjects and soft backgrounds. Bring a hat, sunscreen, binoculars and a camera strap. The light bouncing off the lake can be strong even on cool mornings." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/hippos-river-safari-simbali-wild-safaris.jpg", alt: "Hippos resting in water during an East African safari", caption: "Naivasha's hippos are best admired from a guided boat at a safe distance." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Crescent Island: Walking Among Wildlife" },
    { type: "text", content: "<strong>Crescent Island Game Sanctuary</strong> is one of Naivasha's quiet treasures. Reached by boat from the eastern side of the lake, it offers a walking safari without large predators. That changes everything: instead of watching from a vehicle, you walk with a guide through open grassland as giraffes, zebras, impalas, waterbuck, wildebeest and gazelles move around you." },
    { type: "text", content: "The pace is gentle, the views are wide, and the experience is especially memorable for families and honeymoon couples. Children love the immediacy of walking near giraffes; couples love the sense of privacy and the cinematic sweep of the lake, volcanoes and Rift Valley wall." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/giraffes-savanna-safari-simbali-wild-safaris.jpg", alt: "Giraffes walking across open savanna", caption: "Crescent Island turns wildlife viewing into a gentle walking safari." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Hell's Gate: Cliffs, Cycling and Geothermal Drama" },
    { type: "text", content: "Just a short drive from Naivasha, <strong>Hell's Gate National Park</strong> adds a completely different kind of adventure. Its red cliffs, volcanic plugs and geothermal steam vents make it one of Kenya's most dramatic landscapes. It is also one of the rare parks where visitors can cycle among wildlife such as zebra, giraffe, eland, buffalo and baboons." },
    { type: "text", content: "A beautifully paced day can begin with a sunrise boat safari, continue with a Crescent Island walk, and end with cycling or a geothermal spa soak near Hell's Gate. It is active without feeling rushed, and it gives travelers a tactile sense of the Rift Valley's volcanic energy." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/mountain-hiking-adventure-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Travelers hiking through a dramatic Rift Valley landscape", caption: "Hell's Gate brings cliffs, walking and cycling into a Naivasha safari stay." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Where Naivasha Fits in a Kenya Safari" },
    { type: "text", content: "We include Lake Naivasha in both romantic and family-focused Kenya journeys because it balances the safari beautifully. After the arid textures of Samburu or the conservation story of Ol Pejeta, the lake feels cool and spacious. Before or after the Maasai Mara, it gives travelers time to breathe, watch, listen and reset." },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Kenya Honeymoon 8-Day:</strong> Day 5 brings couples from Ol Pejeta to Lake Naivasha for a peaceful boat safari, lakeside dining and a softer interlude before the Maasai Mara.",
      "<strong>Kenya Family 8-Day:</strong> Day 4 centers on Naivasha's boat safari, where children can spot hippos, pelicans and fish eagles from the water before continuing to Lake Nakuru and the Mara.",
      "<strong>Cross-country combinations:</strong> Naivasha also works well as a gentle first stop from Nairobi before longer Kenya-Tanzania safari circuits.",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "Best Time to Visit Lake Naivasha" },
    { type: "text", content: "Naivasha is rewarding year-round. Birding is especially rich from <strong>October to April</strong>, when migrants join resident species. The drier months of July to October and January to March often make wildlife viewing easier around the shore, while rainy periods bring lush scenery and fuller papyrus habitat." },
    { type: "text", content: "April and May can bring heavier rains, so the surrounding roads and tracks may need more flexibility. Even then, the lake rarely loses its appeal; mist, cloud and fresh green growth can make the landscape feel wonderfully alive." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Practical Tips for a Naivasha Stay" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Pack binoculars even if you are not a dedicated birder; Naivasha has a way of converting casual wildlife lovers.",
      "Choose early morning or late afternoon for the most atmospheric boat safari light.",
      "Wear neutral clothing and comfortable walking shoes for Crescent Island.",
      "Keep a respectful distance from hippos and follow your guide's instructions at all times.",
      "Ask about flower farm visits if you are interested in the lake's conservation and community story.",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "A Gentle Invitation" },
    { type: "text", content: "Lake Naivasha is not a lesser safari because it is quiet. Its magic is in the softness: the fish eagle call, the ripple of a hippo surfacing, the hush of a boat cutting through morning mist, and the joy of walking among giraffes on Crescent Island." },
    { type: "text", content: "Let Sheila and Violet at <strong>Simbali Wild Safaris</strong> weave Naivasha's serenity into your perfect Kenya journey. Explore our <a href=\"/itineraries#kenya-honeymoon-8\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">8-Day Kenya Honeymoon Safari</a> and <a href=\"/itineraries#kenya-family-8\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">8-Day Kenya Family Safari</a>, or <a href=\"/contact\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">contact us</a> for a bespoke lakeside safari experience." },
  ],
  "wonders-of-kenyas-great-rift-valley": [
    { type: "text", content: "The <strong>Great Rift Valley Kenya</strong> is one of the most incredible places on Earth. It's part of a massive crack in the planet's surface that runs from the Middle East all the way down through Eastern Africa. The Kenyan part of the valley is especially beautiful — and full of surprises." },
    { type: "text", content: "Here, you'll find tall cliffs called escarpments, wide lakes, hot springs, volcanoes, and endless plains. It's a place where nature tells the story of our planet's past — and gives you plenty to explore today." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Nature's Artwork: Lakes, Volcanoes, and More" },
    { type: "text", content: "The landscapes of the Great Rift Valley Kenya are full of variety:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Lake Naivasha</strong> is peaceful and great for boat rides.",
      "<strong>Lake Nakuru</strong> often turns pink from thousands of flamingos.",
      "<strong>Lake Bogoria</strong> is known for its hot springs and geysers.",
      "<strong>Mount Longonot</strong>, an extinct volcano, offers amazing views after a good hike.",
      "<strong>Hell's Gate National Park</strong> has deep gorges and steam vents — and you can walk or cycle among wild animals!",
    ]},
    { type: "image", src: "/images/serene-lakeside-mountain-landscape_blog.jpg", alt: "Serene lakeside with mountain landscape in the Great Rift Valley", caption: "The dramatic landscapes of the Great Rift Valley are shaped by millions of years of geological activity" },
    { type: "text", content: "These places aren't just pretty — they were shaped by powerful forces millions of years ago. Scientists say the African continent is slowly splitting apart, and the Rift Valley shows us how that's happening." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Wildlife Everywhere You Look" },
    { type: "text", content: "The Great Rift Valley is a dream destination for animal lovers. Its lakes, forests, grasslands, and wetlands create homes for many species." },
    { type: "text", content: "Here's what you might see:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Flamingos, pelicans, and over 400 bird species",
      "Giraffes, zebras, and antelope grazing freely",
      "Lions, leopards, and elephants in nearby reserves like <strong>Lake Nakuru National Park</strong> and <strong>Maasai Mara</strong>",
      "Hippos and crocodiles in Lake Naivasha",
    ]},
    { type: "text", content: "And yes, the world-famous <strong>Great Migration</strong> sometimes edges into the valley's plains as wildebeests and zebras move in massive herds." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/flamingos-wetland-habitat-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Flock of pink flamingos in their wetland habitat", caption: "Lake Nakuru's flamingo colonies are one of the Rift Valley's most iconic sights" },
    { type: "h2", content: "Culture and People of the Valley" },
    { type: "text", content: "The people of the <strong>Great Rift Valley Kenya</strong> are just as special as the land itself. Many communities live here, each with their own language, stories, and ways of life." },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "The <strong>Maasai</strong> are known worldwide for their red clothing, beadwork, and warrior dances.",
      "The <strong>Kalenjin</strong> are legendary runners — you may have heard of them winning Olympic gold!",
      "The <strong>Turkana</strong> live near Lake Turkana and have a rich culture of fishing, storytelling, and crafts.",
      "The <strong>Samburu</strong> are pastoralists who raise cattle and keep traditional customs alive.",
    ]},
    { type: "text", content: "Some of the most important discoveries about early human life have also come from this region. Fossils found in places like <strong>Koobi Fora</strong> show that humans have been living in the Rift Valley for millions of years. That's why it's often called the \"Cradle of Humankind.\"" },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/maasai-cultural-experience-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Maasai warriors in traditional dress performing a cultural dance", caption: "The Maasai people are one of the Rift Valley's most iconic communities" },
    { type: "h2", content: "What You Can Do There: Adventure & Relaxation" },
    { type: "text", content: "No matter what kind of traveler you are, the Great Rift Valley has something for you:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Hiking:</strong> Climb Mount Longonot or trek through Hell's Gate's gorges",
      "<strong>Birdwatching:</strong> Visit Lakes Baringo and Nakuru",
      "<strong>Boating:</strong> Glide past hippos on Lake Naivasha",
      "<strong>Camping:</strong> Stay in eco-lodges or set up a tent under the stars",
      "<strong>Cultural tours:</strong> Spend a day with local communities and learn from their way of life",
      "<strong>Relaxing:</strong> Soak in hot springs at Lake Bogoria or watch a sunset over the escarpment",
    ]},
    { type: "text", content: "There are many <strong>community-run camps</strong> and eco-friendly stays that make your trip more meaningful while supporting the people who live there." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/mountain-hiking-adventure-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Hikers trekking through the dramatic landscape of the Rift Valley", caption: "From mountain hikes to boat safaris, the Rift Valley offers adventure for everyone" },
    { type: "h2", content: "Snapshots of a Magical Place" },
    { type: "text", content: "If you're into photography or just love beautiful views, this is your place:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Sunrise lighting up the Mau Escarpment",
      "Pink flamingos flying over a lake",
      "A lion resting in golden grass",
      "Maasai warriors dancing in full regalia",
    ]},
    { type: "text", content: "Whether you have a fancy camera or just a smartphone, the <strong>Great Rift Valley Kenya</strong> is one of the most photogenic places in Africa." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Caring for the Valley" },
    { type: "text", content: "As amazing as the valley is, it's also facing big challenges. Climate change, deforestation, pollution, and human-wildlife conflict all threaten the land and the animals that live there." },
    { type: "text", content: "Thankfully, groups like <strong>The Green Belt Movement</strong>, started by Nobel Prize winner <strong>Wangari Maathai</strong>, are working to plant trees, protect forests, and teach sustainable farming." },
    { type: "text", content: "When you visit, you can help too — by choosing eco-friendly options, respecting local communities, and leaving nature as you found it." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Final Thoughts: Why It Matters" },
    { type: "text", content: "The <strong>Great Rift Valley Kenya</strong> isn't just a place on the map. It's a living story — about the Earth's power, the beauty of nature, the strength of cultures, and the hope for a better future." },
    { type: "text", content: "Whether you explore it in person or simply dream about it, we hope this journey inspires you to learn more, travel wisely, and care deeply about this incredible part of our world." },
    { type: "text", content: "At <strong>Simbali Wild Safaris</strong>, we can help you plan the perfect trip — whether you want a quick day visit or a longer adventure exploring the lakes and parks. <a href=\"/contact\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">Contact us today</a> and let us take you to one of Kenya's most amazing natural wonders!" },
  ],
  "east-africa-birding-guide": [
    { type: "text", content: "East Africa is a birdwatcher's paradise, bursting with life and color. With over <strong>1,500 bird species</strong>, the region is home to about <strong>10% of the world's bird population</strong>. Whether you're a seasoned birder or just curious about wildlife, <strong>East Africa birding</strong> offers an unforgettable experience." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/birds-flying-over-grassland-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Flock of birds flying over the golden grasslands of East Africa", caption: "East Africa's diverse habitats support an extraordinary variety of bird species" },
    { type: "h2", content: "Why East Africa Is a Birdwatcher's Paradise" },
    { type: "text", content: "East Africa's diverse geography — from vast savannas and alpine forests to wetlands and tropical rainforests — provides an incredible range of habitats for birds. It's also strategically located along the <strong>Afro-Palearctic migration route</strong>, making it a critical stopover for millions of migratory birds traveling between Europe, Asia, and Southern Africa." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Key Migration Routes" },
    { type: "text", content: "One of the most fascinating birdwatching spectacles is migration. The <strong>Great Rift Valley</strong>, stretching from Lebanon to Mozambique, serves as a major flyway for birds. During migration seasons, birdwatchers can spot species such as:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Amur Falcon</strong>",
      "<strong>Lesser Kestrel</strong>",
      "<strong>European Roller</strong>",
      "<strong>White Stork</strong>",
    ]},
    { type: "text", content: "Bird migration peaks between <strong>September and April</strong>, making it an ideal time to plan a birding trip." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Endemic Treasures" },
    { type: "text", content: "The region also hosts over <strong>150 endemic bird species</strong>, found nowhere else on Earth. These birds have evolved in isolation in unique microclimates and habitats. Highlights include:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Taita White-eye</strong> — native to the Taita Hills in Kenya",
      "<strong>Aberdare Cisticola</strong> — only found in Kenya's Aberdare range",
      "<strong>Kilimanjaro White-eye</strong> — exclusive to the slopes of Mount Kilimanjaro",
    ]},
    { type: "image", src: "/images/songbird-perched-on-branch-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "A beautiful songbird perched on a branch in East Africa", caption: "Endemic species like the Taita White-eye can only be found in specific East African locations" },
    { type: "h2", content: "Kenya: The Crown Jewel of African Birding" },
    { type: "text", content: "Kenya is often considered the ultimate birdwatching destination. With over <strong>1,100 bird species</strong>, including 60 endemics, the country offers a mix of accessible birding spots, great infrastructure, and experienced guides." },
    { type: "h3", content: "Lake Nakuru National Park" },
    { type: "text", content: "Renowned for its spectacular <strong>flamingo flocks</strong>, Lake Nakuru is also home to more than 400 bird species. Key sightings include:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Lesser Flamingo",
      "Greater Flamingo",
      "African Fish Eagle",
    ]},
    { type: "image", src: "/images/flamingos-wetland-habitat-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Thousands of pink flamingos at Lake Nakuru", caption: "Lake Nakuru's flamingo flocks are one of Africa's most spectacular birding sights" },
    { type: "h3", content: "Aberdare National Park" },
    { type: "text", content: "Located in central Kenya, this park features misty forests and cool mountain air. It's a haven for highland forest birds such as:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Aberdare Cisticola",
      "Mountain Yellow Warbler",
      "Hunter's Cisticola",
    ]},
    { type: "h3", content: "Other Top Spots in Kenya" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Tsavo National Park</strong> — Great for raptors and ground hornbills",
      "<strong>Hell's Gate National Park</strong> — Unique landscapes and cliff-dwelling species",
      "<strong>Kakamega Forest</strong> — The only tropical rainforest in Kenya",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "Tanzania: From Serengeti Skies to Hidden Forests" },
    { type: "text", content: "Tanzania is famed for the <strong>Serengeti</strong> and <strong>Mount Kilimanjaro</strong>, but its birdlife is equally impressive. With over 1,000 species, the country's birding destinations are as diverse as its landscapes." },
    { type: "h3", content: "Serengeti National Park" },
    { type: "text", content: "Known for the Great Migration, the Serengeti also hosts:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Karamoja Apalis",
      "Red-throated Tit",
      "Rufous-tailed Weaver",
    ]},
    { type: "h3", content: "Tarangire National Park" },
    { type: "text", content: "Home to majestic baobabs and large elephant herds, this park also attracts colorful birds such as:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Yellow-collared Lovebird",
      "Red-billed Buffalo Weaver",
      "White-headed Barbet",
    ]},
    { type: "image", src: "/images/turaco-bird-branch-wildlife-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "A colorful turaco bird perched on a branch", caption: "Tanzania's forests are home to spectacular species like the turaco" },
    { type: "h3", content: "Other Top Spots in Tanzania" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Ngorongoro Crater</strong> — A biodiversity hotspot",
      "<strong>Lake Manyara</strong> — Known for flamingos and waterfowl",
      "<strong>Rubondo Island</strong> — Rare island birding experience",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "Uganda: The Realm of the Shoebill" },
    { type: "text", content: "Uganda's lush wetlands, forests, and lakes make it a top birding location. With over 1,000 species, it's the best place in the world to see the rare and prehistoric-looking <strong>Shoebill Stork</strong>." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/shoebill-bird-wildlife-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "The prehistoric-looking Shoebill Stork in Uganda's wetlands", caption: "The Shoebill Stork is one of Africa's most sought-after bird sightings" },
    { type: "h3", content: "Mabamba Swamp" },
    { type: "text", content: "Located near Entebbe on the shores of Lake Victoria, this swamp is a <strong>must-visit</strong> for Shoebill sightings. Other notable species include:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Blue-headed Coucal",
      "Blue-breasted Bee-eater",
      "White-winged Warbler",
    ]},
    { type: "h3", content: "Bwindi Impenetrable National Park" },
    { type: "text", content: "A UNESCO World Heritage Site, Bwindi is not just for gorilla trekking — it also offers spectacular birding with over <strong>350 species</strong>:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Chapin's Flycatcher",
      "Short-tailed Warbler",
      "Kivu Ground Thrush",
    ]},
    { type: "h3", content: "More Birding Hotspots in Uganda" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Queen Elizabeth National Park</strong>",
      "<strong>Kibale Forest</strong> — Famous for chimpanzees and birds",
      "<strong>Semuliki National Park</strong> — Rich in Central African bird species",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "Rwanda & Burundi: Forest Jewels of the Great Lakes" },
    { type: "text", content: "Though often overlooked, <strong>Rwanda</strong> and <strong>Burundi</strong> offer rewarding birding in compact and diverse areas." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/small-bird-nature-watching-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "A small colorful bird in the forests of Rwanda", caption: "Rwanda's Nyungwe Forest is home to over 300 bird species" },
    { type: "h3", content: "Nyungwe National Park (Rwanda)" },
    { type: "text", content: "One of Africa's oldest rainforests, Nyungwe is home to over <strong>300 bird species</strong>, including:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Red-collared Turaco",
      "Ruwenzori Blue Turaco",
      "Yellow-crested Helmetshrike",
    ]},
    { type: "h3", content: "Akagera National Park (Rwanda)" },
    { type: "text", content: "Akagera is ideal for wetland and savanna birds. Notable species:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Shoebill",
      "Saddle-billed Stork",
      "African Skimmer",
    ]},
    { type: "h3", content: "Birding in Burundi" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Ruvubu National Park</strong> — Largest national park in Burundi",
      "<strong>Kibira National Park</strong> — Montane forest with rich birdlife",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "Planning Your East African Birding Adventure" },
    { type: "text", content: "A successful East Africa birding trip depends on good planning." },
    { type: "h3", content: "Best Times to Go" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Dry seasons</strong> (December–March and June–September) are best for visibility and travel ease",
      "<strong>Migration periods</strong> offer chances to see rare migrants",
    ]},
    { type: "h3", content: "Birding Essentials" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Binoculars (8x42 recommended)",
      "Field guide — <em>Birds of East Africa</em> by Stevenson & Fanshawe",
      "Camera with zoom lens",
      "Notebook or birding app for logging sightings",
    ]},
    { type: "h3", content: "Hire a Local Guide" },
    { type: "text", content: "Local bird guides know the best spots, calls, and behaviors. At <strong>Simbali Wild Safaris</strong>, our expert guides can help you identify the hot spots and make the most of your birding adventure." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Safety and Travel Tips" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "Check visa requirements for each country",
      "Ensure vaccinations (Yellow Fever, Hepatitis, etc.)",
      "Pack light but bring layers for varying climates",
      "Stay in eco-lodges for sustainable travel",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "Conclusion" },
    { type: "text", content: "Whether you're hoping to glimpse the <strong>Shoebill</strong> in Uganda, watch flamingos at Lake Nakuru, or track endemics in the Taita Hills, <strong>East Africa birding</strong> is truly a dream experience." },
    { type: "text", content: "So pack your binoculars, download your birding checklist, and get ready to explore one of the richest birding regions on the planet! <a href=\"/contact\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">Contact Simbali Wild Safaris</a> to plan your ultimate birding adventure." },
  ],
  "wildebeest-migration-an-epic-safari-adventure": [
    { type: "text", content: "Every year, over a million wildebeest embark on a treacherous journey across the vast Serengeti-Mara ecosystem in Tanzania and Kenya. The Great Migration is a breathtaking journey many dream of seeing in person. It's truly a <strong>once-in-a-lifetime experience</strong>." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/wildebeest-migration-herd-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Massive herd of wildebeest stretching across the golden Serengeti plains", caption: "The sheer scale of the migration — over 1.5 million animals on the move" },
    { type: "h2", content: "Understanding the Wildebeest Migration Route" },
    { type: "text", content: "The wildebeest migration is a continuous cyclical journey, driven by the ever-changing availability of water and fresh grazing grounds. These animals travel over <strong>1,800 miles</strong> across Tanzania's Serengeti and Kenya's Maasai Mara." },
    { type: "text", content: "The migration begins in southern Serengeti around January. As thousands of wildebeest give birth during the calving season — a breathtaking sight." },
    { type: "text", content: "As the rains fade and grass thins out, the wildebeest move north in search of fresh greenery. Their journey leads them across dangerous rivers, where they face strong currents and the constant threat of crocodiles." },
    { type: "image", src: "/images/wildebeest-rocky-river-crossing-safari_blog.jpg", alt: "Dramatic wildebeest crossing a rocky river during the migration", caption: "The river crossings are the most dramatic — and dangerous — part of the migration" },
    { type: "h2", content: "The Great Migration: A Life-or-Death Journey" },
    { type: "text", content: "The wildebeest migration is more than a scenic journey. It's a life-or-death trek that highlights nature's raw power and the fight for survival. Along the way, the wildebeest face numerous dangers and obstacles that test their endurance and resilience." },
    { type: "text", content: "One of the most iconic and heart-wrenching events of the migration is the river crossings. As the herds approach the swollen rivers, a palpable tension builds, and the wildebeest hesitate, sensing the danger that lies ahead. Eventually, the sheer weight of the massive herd pushes the first brave individuals into the water, and the rest follow in a frenzied stampede." },
    { type: "text", content: "The river crossings are a true spectacle of nature's drama, with wildebeest struggling against the powerful currents, while crocodiles and other predators lie in wait, ready to claim their share of the bounty. The sheer chaos and intensity of these crossings are both awe-inspiring and heart-wrenching, leaving spectators on the edge of their seats." },
    { type: "text", content: "Beyond the river crossings, the wildebeest must also contend with other formidable obstacles, such as steep ravines, rugged terrain, and the ever-present threat of predators like lions, hyenas, and cheetahs. These skilled hunters take advantage of the vulnerability of the wildebeest during their journey, picking off the weak, the young, and the stragglers in a never-ending cycle of life and death." },
    { type: "h2", content: "When to Witness the Wildebeest Migration" },
    { type: "text", content: "Timing is crucial when it comes to witnessing the wildebeest migration in all its glory. The migration is a year-round event, but certain periods offer more spectacular sights than others." },
    { type: "h3", content: "The Calving Season (January – March)" },
    { type: "text", content: "The calving season, which typically occurs between late January and early March, is an extraordinary time to witness the wildebeest migration. During this period, hundreds of thousands of wildebeest congregate in the southern plains of the Serengeti to give birth to their calves. The sight of newborn calves taking their first wobbly steps amidst the vast herds is a truly heartwarming and unforgettable experience." },
    { type: "h3", content: "The River Crossings (June – July)" },
    { type: "text", content: "One of the most dramatic and highly anticipated events of the migration is the river crossings, which typically occur between June and July. During this time, the wildebeest herds must navigate the treacherous waters of the Mara River, braving powerful currents and the ever-present threat of crocodile attacks. The sheer intensity and chaos of these crossings make for an adrenaline-pumping and unforgettable experience." },
    { type: "h3", content: "The Northward Trek (August – October)" },
    { type: "text", content: "As the dry season sets in, the wildebeest begin their northward journey towards the lush grazing grounds of the Maasai Mara National Reserve in Kenya. This period, from August to October, offers ample opportunities to witness the massive herds on the move, grazing in the open plains and interacting with other iconic African wildlife species." },
    { type: "h2", content: "Planning Your Wildebeest Migration Safari" },
    { type: "text", content: "Witnessing the wildebeest migration is a bucket list experience for many, but it requires careful planning and preparation. Here are some tips to help you make the most of your wildebeest migration safari:" },
    { type: "list", items: [
      "<strong>Choose the Right Time:</strong> Decide on the specific event or phase of the migration you wish to witness, and plan your trip accordingly. Keep in mind that the migration is a fluid event, and the timing can vary from year to year.",
      "<strong>Book Early:</strong> Popular safari camps and lodges in the Serengeti and Maasai Mara can fill up quickly, especially during peak migration periods. Use our <a href=\"/services\" class=\"text-olive underline\">Tailored Safari packages</a> and Book well in advance to secure your spot.",
      "<strong>Hire a Knowledgeable Guide:</strong> Our skill and experience safari guide can make all the difference in your migration experience. We will not only help you navigate the best viewing spots but also provide invaluable insights into the behavior and dynamics of the wildebeest herds.",
      "<strong>Pack Appropriately:</strong> The Serengeti and Maasai Mara can be hot and dusty during the dry season, and the terrain can be rugged. Pack lightweight, breathable clothing, sturdy hiking shoes, and plenty of sunscreen and insect repellent.",
      "<strong>Be Prepared for Long Days:</strong> Witnessing the wildebeest migration often involves long hours in a safari vehicle, navigating rough terrain and waiting patiently for the herds to move or for river crossings to occur. Be prepared for long days and early starts to make the most of your <a href=\"/itineraries\" class=\"text-olive underline\">Wonders of the Natural World</a>.",
      "<strong>Respect the Environment:</strong> The wildebeest migration is a fragile and delicate ecosystem. Follow all guidelines and regulations set by the national parks and your safari operators to minimize your impact on the environment and the wildlife.",
    ]},
    { type: "h2", content: "Conservation and the Future of the Migration" },
    { type: "text", content: "The Great Wildebeest Migration isn't just an amazing sight — it's a key part of keeping the Serengeti-Mara ecosystem alive and healthy. Every year, over a million wildebeests move across the plains, supporting a wide variety of animals, from lions and hyenas to birds and insects." },
    { type: "text", content: "But this natural wonder is under threat. Habitat loss, poaching, and climate change are putting pressure on the migration routes. As nearby human populations grow, the wildebeests' grazing lands and paths are shrinking, causing more conflict and damage to the ecosystem." },
    { type: "text", content: "Thankfully, organizations like the <strong>Frankfurt Zoological Society</strong>, the <strong>Serengeti Wildlife Research Institute</strong>, and <strong>Tanzania's National Parks Authority</strong> are working hard to protect the migration. They run anti-poaching patrols, educate local communities, and help create safe corridors for the animals to travel." },
    { type: "text", content: "Still, they can't do it alone. Protecting the wildebeest migration depends on support from people like you — travelers, nature lovers, and anyone who cares about our planet." },
    { type: "text", content: "When you book your safari with eco-friendly tour operators and respect the wildlife, you're helping preserve this incredible event." },
    { type: "text", content: "Your trip becomes more than just a once-in-a-lifetime adventure — it becomes part of the effort to keep the wildebeest migration alive for future generations." },
    { type: "text", content: "At <strong>Simbali Wild Safaris</strong>, we believe in responsible tourism that respects wildlife and supports local communities. We follow strict guidelines to minimize our impact, support conservation efforts, and ensure that the migration can be enjoyed by future generations. <a href=\"/contact\" class=\"text-olive underline font-semibold\">Contact us today</a> to start planning your migration safari!" },
  ],
};

const { slug } = Astro.params;
const { post } = Astro.props;
const content = blogContent[slug] || [];
const related = relatedSafaris[slug] || [];
const allSlugs = ["maasai-culture-safari-east-africa", "lake-naivasha-kenya-boat-safari-birdwatching", "wonders-of-kenyas-great-rift-valley", "east-africa-birding-guide", "wildebeest-migration-an-epic-safari-adventure"];
const otherSlugs = allSlugs.filter(s => s !== slug);
---

<MainLayout
  title={`${post.title} | Simbali Wild Safaris Blog`}
  description={post.description}
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="canonical" href={`https://simbaliwildsafaris.com/blog/${slug}/`} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={`${post.title} | Simbali Wild Safaris`} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={post.description} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:url" content={`https://simbaliwildsafaris.com/blog/${slug}/`} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={`https://simbaliwildsafaris.com${post.img}`} />
    <meta property="article:published_time" content={post.date} />
    <meta property="article:section" content={post.cat} />
    {post.tags.map(tag => <meta property="article:tag" content={tag} />)}
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "BlogPosting",
      "headline": post.title,
      "description": post.description,
      "image": `https://simbaliwildsafaris.com${post.img}`,
      "datePublished": post.date,
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": post.author },
      "publisher": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "Simbali Wild Safaris",
        "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://simbaliwildsafaris.com/images/simbali-favicon.png" }
      },
      "mainEntityOfPage": {
        "@type": "WebPage",
        "@id": `https://simbaliwildsafaris.com/blog/${slug}/`
      }
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  {/* Hero */}
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0">
      <OptimizedImage src={post.img} alt={post.imgAlt} class="w-full h-full object-cover" loading="eager" />
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-black/50" />
    </div>
    <div class="relative container-main py-20 md:py-28">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center anim-fade-up">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 text-white/70 text-sm mb-4">
          <span class="bg-white/20 backdrop-blur px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider">{post.cat}</span>
          <span>{post.date}</span>
          <span>/</span>
          <span>{post.readTime}</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-belleza text-3xl md:text-5xl tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-white leading-tight mb-4">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="text-white/80 text-base md:text-lg max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">{post.description}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Article */}
  <section class="py-16 bg-white">
    <div class="container-main max-w-4xl">
      <article class="prose-blog max-w-none">
        {content.map((block) => {
          if (block.type === 'h2') return <h2 class="font-belleza text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-[0.08em] uppercase mt-12 mb-5 text-[#1a1a1a]" set:html={block.content} />;
          if (block.type === 'h3') return <h3 class="font-belleza text-xl md:text-2xl tracking-[0.06em] uppercase mt-8 mb-4 text-[#1a1a1a]" set:html={block.content} />;
          if (block.type === 'text') return <p class="text-[#444] leading-[1.8] mb-5 text-[15px]" set:html={block.content} />;
          if (block.type === 'quote') return (
            <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-olive bg-sage/20 pl-6 pr-4 py-5 my-8 rounded-r-xl">
              <p class="text-[#555] italic text-lg leading-relaxed">"{block.content}"</p>
            </blockquote>
          );
          if (block.type === 'list') return (
            <ul class="space-y-3 mb-6">
              {block.items?.map(item => <li class="text-[#444] text-[15px] leading-[1.8] flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-olive flex-shrink-0 mt-1.5 text-xs">&#9654;</span><span set:html={item} /></li>)}
            </ul>
          );
          if (block.type === 'image') return (
            <figure class="my-8 anim-scale-in">
              <OptimizedImage src={block.src} alt={block.alt} class="w-full h-64 md:h-80 object-cover rounded-xl" loading="lazy" />
              {block.caption && <figcaption class="text-center text-[#999] text-sm mt-2 italic">{block.caption}</figcaption>}
            </figure>
          );
          return null;
        })}
      </article>

      {/* Tags */}
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-10 pt-8 border-t border-gray-100">
        {post.tags.map(tag => <span class="text-xs text-[#666] bg-[#f5f7f3] px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full">{tag}</span>)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Related Safaris */}
  {related.length > 0 && (
    <section class="py-16 bg-sage/20">
      <div class="container-main max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="font-belleza text-2xl tracking-[0.1em] uppercase mb-8 text-center anim-fade-up">Related Safari Itineraries</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          {related.map((safari, i) => (
            <a href={safari.url} class={`group bg-white rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 hover:shadow-lg transition-all duration-300 flex flex-col hover-lift anim-fade-up anim-delay-${i + 1}`}>
              <div class="relative overflow-hidden h-48">
                <OptimizedImage src={safari.img} alt={safari.title} class="w-full h-full object-cover group-hover:scale-105 transition-transform duration-500" loading="lazy" />
                <div class="absolute top-3 right-3 bg-white/90 backdrop-blur text-olive text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">{safari.duration}</div>
              </div>
              <div class="p-5 flex flex-col flex-1">
                <h3 class="font-belleza text-lg tracking-[0.06em] uppercase mb-1">{safari.title}</h3>
                <p class="text-olive font-semibold text-sm">{safari.price}</p>
                <span class="text-olive text-xs font-semibold mt-3 group-hover:underline inline-flex items-center gap-1">View Itinerary <span>&rarr;</span></span>
              </div>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  {/* More Stories */}
  <section class="py-16 bg-white">
    <div class="container-main max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-belleza text-2xl tracking-[0.1em] uppercase mb-8 text-center anim-fade-up">More Safari Stories</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        {otherSlugs.map((otherSlug, idx) => {
          const otherPosts = {
            "maasai-culture-safari-east-africa": { title: "Maasai Culture Safari Guide", img: "/images/maasai-cultural-experience-safari_blog.jpg", excerpt: "Visit Maasai communities with respect, context and a deeper understanding of beadwork, dance and land." },
            "lake-naivasha-kenya-boat-safari-birdwatching": { title: "Lake Naivasha: Boat Safari & Birdwatching", img: "/images/serene-lakeside-mountain-landscape_blog.jpg", excerpt: "Drift past hippos, fish eagles and Crescent Island wildlife on Kenya's highest lake." },
            "wonders-of-kenyas-great-rift-valley": { title: "Great Rift Valley Kenya: Nature, Culture & Adventure", img: "/images/serene-lakeside-mountain-landscape_blog.jpg", excerpt: "Discover dramatic escarpments, flamingo-filled lakes and volcanic craters." },
            "east-africa-birding-guide": { title: "East Africa Birding Guide", img: "/images/raptor-bird-flying-clear-sky_blog.jpg", excerpt: "Over 1,500 bird species across Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda and beyond." },
            "wildebeest-migration-an-epic-safari-adventure": { title: "The Great Wildebeest Migration", img: "/images/wildebeest-river-crossing-safari-migration_blog.jpg", excerpt: "Witness over a million wildebeest thunder across the Serengeti-Maasai Mara." },
          };
          const op = otherPosts[otherSlug];
          return (
            <a href={`/blog/${otherSlug}`} class={`group flex gap-4 items-start hover:bg-sage/10 p-3 rounded-xl transition-colors anim-fade-up anim-delay-${idx + 1}`}>
              <OptimizedImage src={op.img} alt={op.title} class="w-24 h-24 rounded-lg object-cover flex-shrink-0 group-hover:scale-105 transition-transform" loading="lazy" />
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-belleza text-base tracking-[0.06em] uppercase leading-snug mb-1 group-hover:text-olive transition-colors">{op.title}</h3>
                <p class="text-[#777] text-sm leading-relaxed">{op.excerpt}</p>
              </div>
            </a>
          );
        })}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* CTA */}
  <section class="py-20 bg-olive">
    <div class="container-main text-center anim-fade-up">
      <h2 class="font-belleza text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-[0.1em] uppercase text-white mb-4">Ready to Experience This?</h2>
      <p class="text-white/70 max-w-xl mx-auto mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        Let Simbali Wild Safaris craft your perfect African adventure. From the Great Migration to the Great Rift Valley, we'll design a journey you'll never forget.
      </p>
      <a href="/contact" class="btn-primary btn-pulse bg-white text-olive hover:bg-sage inline-flex">Plan Your Safari</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</MainLayout>
